  "summary": "New preprints show global correlation and plate layout inflate perturbation-prediction benchmarks, while context-explicit and measurement-grounded models emerge.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether single-cell perturbation-response predictions are reliable, and how evaluation design shapes the answer. The Delta Gap preprint reports GEARS at delta Pearson 0.076 versus 0.267 for a mean-delta baseline on Replogle K562, indicating global Pearson inflates apparent performance. A separate preprint shows plate layout and well effects confound donor variation and benchmark evaluation, and proposes design-aware estimators. In parallel, methods make context explicit or ground predictions in measurements: UnDetect separates cellular context from compound effects with multi-view fusion, BioMeme uses a memory of measured experiments, a kernel HSIC regularizer targets nonlinear disentanglement, and prior-knowledge agreement tracks target-level predictability. Patient-derived organoid screens add benchmarking data for drug response.",
